{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    // The app is a private, non-published frontend (consumed by the CLI as built
    // assets, never as .d.ts). It emits no declarations, so the base config's
    // declaration/declarationMap only trigger .d.ts portability checks (TS4058/
    // TS2742) with no output benefit — and pnpm's isolated node_modules encodes
    // peer-resolved dependency realpaths under .pnpm/ that those checks reject as
    // "not portable". Turning declarations off scopes those checks to the packages
    // that actually publish types; full --noEmit type checking still runs.
    "declaration": false,
    "declarationMap": false,
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    }
  },
  // `tests/half-open-forced-close.test-helper.ts` is in the program on purpose:
  // it reads `@hocuspocus/provider` internals that four suites arrange through,
  // so a bump that renames one of them has to fail here as one compile error
  // rather than at runtime inside four unrelated-looking test files.
  //
  // What that include actually pulls in is the helper PLUS its import closure,
  // notably `tests/integration/test-harness.ts`, because TypeScript follows
  // imports and `exclude` only filters the `*.test.ts`/`*.test.tsx` entry
  // points. That is the deliberate cost of typechecking the helper, not an
  // oversight. Naming further files here is a separate change.
  //
  // `tests/stress/_helpers/image.ts` is named for the same reason: its
  // `instanceof HTMLImageElement` narrowing is what gives the polled element
  // `.complete`/`.naturalWidth`, since Playwright types `evaluate`'s argument
  // as `SVGElement | HTMLElement`. Nothing else typechecks the E2E helpers, so
  // without this, deleting that narrowing compiles silently; with it, deleting
  // it fails as TS2339 on both properties. That is the whole guarantee: a cast
  // to `HTMLImageElement` still compiles (a downcast within the union is
  // permitted), and the `.complete` conjunct is not pinned. It imports only
  // `@playwright/test`, so its closure adds no other file to the program.
  //
  // `tests/stress/_helpers/theme-fade.ts` is named for the same reason, and its
  // guarantee is narrower. It pins the transition probe's own types: the
  // `Capture` discriminated union that makes a matched capture missing its
  // `transition`, `durationMs` or `delayMs` unrepresentable, the
  // `transitionFor` type predicate that keeps `getAnimations().find(...)`
  // returning `CSSTransition | undefined` with no cast at the use site, and the
  // `typeof duration !== 'number'` and `typeof delay !== 'number'` rejections
  // that narrow what `getComputedTiming()` gives those two: respectively a
  // `string | number | CSSNumericValue | undefined` union and an optional
  // `number`. Delete any of them and this compile fails inside `armThemeFade`.
  // As with `image.ts` above, replacing one with a cast still compiles: the pin
  // is against silent removal, not against a deliberate assertion. The
  // guarantee also stops at the module boundary: the three `*.e2e.ts` call
  // sites stay outside the program, so their
  // `window as typeof window & ThemeFadeProbeWindow` casts and their reads off
  // the returned report are unchecked — which is why the color-theme switch
  // helper rejects an inapplicable theme id by throwing rather than by typing
  // its parameter. It imports `@playwright/test` and
  // `@inkeep/open-knowledge-core`, whose declarations `src` already pulls into
  // the program, so its closure adds no other file to it.
  //
  // `tests/stress/_helpers/editor-state.ts` is named for the same reason. It
  // pins the `CaretPlacement` shape `placeCaretAtEndOfText` returns: four
  // required fields, so the barrier predicate that constructs it fails to
  // compile if a field is dropped or carries the wrong type (TS2322) or is
  // misnamed (TS2561), rather than surfacing as a wrong number at an
  // assertion. The pin stops
  // there: a construction that supplies all four with the right types is
  // accepted whatever the values mean. Playwright's runner transforms
  // `.ts` without typechecking it, so without this entry nothing checks the
  // type the barrier's callers narrow against. It imports only
  // `@playwright/test`, so its closure adds no other file to the program. The
  // `.e2e.ts` and `.dom.test.ts` consumers stay outside: the former would pull
  // the whole `_helpers` barrel in, and the latter is excluded as `*.test.ts`.
  //
  // `tests/integration/audit-barrier.test-helper.ts` is named for the same
  // reason. `docName` is optional upstream, so pushing it unnarrowed into the
  // barrier's `string[]` observation list fails as TS2345 rather than
  // surfacing as an `undefined` at an assertion. The pin stops at the helper:
  // all three files that import it are `*.test.ts` and stay excluded. It
  // imports `@inkeep/open-knowledge-core`, `vitest` and
  // `tests/integration/test-harness.ts`, all of which the entries above
  // already pull in, so its closure adds no other file to the program.
  "include": [
    "src",
    "*.ts",
    "tests/stress/fixtures",
    "tests/half-open-forced-close.test-helper.ts",
    "tests/integration/audit-barrier.test-helper.ts",
    "tests/stress/_helpers/acp-catalog.ts",
    "tests/stress/_helpers/boot-verdict",
    "tests/stress/_helpers/editor-state.ts",
    "tests/stress/_helpers/image.ts",
    "tests/stress/_helpers/theme-fade.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "**/*.test.ts", "**/*.test.tsx"]
}
